  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1032 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nMore unsettled weather is expected into the overnight and Tue as \nthe moisture plume is nudged eastward from AZ by a shortwave \ntrough moving through the Rockies. Upper-level divergence in the \nright entrance region of a jet will allow for some lift tonight \nand convective redevelopment, generally along the RGV. Scattered \nshowers and storms are modeled through midnight according to \nrecent HRRR runs, favoring Sierra and Dona Ana counties. The \nactivity progresses N and dissipates early Tue AM with isolated \nheavy rainfall.\n\nFor Tue afternoon, the moisture plume is over NM (PWs around 1.1\") \nas western areas see more stable air and 500-1000 J/kg CAPE favor E \nof the RGV. Isolated to scattered convection is modeled during the \nafternoon for eastern areas while the rest of the lowlands are \nmainly dry. The highest threat of heavy rainfall will be in the Sacs \nwith slow storm motion to the NE. For Wed, the upper ridge stretches \nacross the southern High Plains, capping storms' ability to fire. \nOnly the high terrain and NM Bootheel have low storm chances Wed \nafternoon.\n\nThu-Sat look more active as subtropical moisture from the Gulf of CA \nis scooped northward by the longwave trough over the Northwest. PWs \nclimb to about 1.3\" (75th-90th %-ile for early Sep) for the lowlands \nwith any embedded shortwaves/vort maxes providing more lift than \njust orographic. Areas W of the RGV look best set up for heavy \nrainfall according to the LREF where moisture levels are higher. \nScattered storms are forecast each day later this week as a result \nof the more favorable monsoon set-up. The upper high looks to drift \nfurther west from the Central US to the southern High Plains early \nnext week based on global ensemble means. This would push much of \nthe moisture into AZ and lower storm chances to end the period.  \n\nTemps will be steady through the weekend with highs a few degrees \nabove normal E of the Divide. The increased clouds and rain chances \nshould lower temps a bit Thu-Sat. Winds remain light through \nmidweek mainly from the W.",
